  "notes": {
    "loss": "Mean loss per token on the held-out verses (5% of each book, at least 50 verses). Tokens are characters plus one tag token per verse, so bpc = nats / ln 2. 'overall' is the mean of the three books, as in training. Every baseline is fitted on the same book's training text. The n-gram baselines use interpolated Witten-Bell smoothing; a stronger smoothing such as Kneser-Ney would lower them further. 'best_baseline' is the lowest of them per book. best.pt was chosen by this same validation loss; there is no separate test split. 'ci95_nats' is a 95% bootstrap interval from resampling held-out verses. Some held-out verses also occur word for word in training (see 'duplicates'); the '_dedup' numbers leave those verses out.",
    "memorisation": "Share of 50-character windows of generated text (tags removed) found verbatim in each book's training text, and in any of them. 'heldout_real' is the same measure on real held-out verses, for reference: scripture repeats itself, so it is not zero.",
    "style": "5-gram classifiers, one per book, each fitted on 92,324 characters of training verses (all of the smallest book, a random subset of the others) without tags; highest likelihood wins. The control rows test the classifier itself: uniform noise should spread over the books, and text with one book's statistics should go to that book. Real held-out chunks overlap by three quarters, so they are not independent. Uniform noise is classified as: gita 22/50, quran 24/50, bible 4/50. unigram text: 63/150 sent to its own book. bigram text: 121/150 sent to its own book. real held-out, 10% of characters corrupted: 263/264 sent to its own book. real held-out text: 264/264 sent to its own book. The classifier is not fully fair: uniform noise goes to bible 4/50 times; unigram text imitating quran is sent to quran only 10/50 times. Read the generated row against these controls.",
    "wellformed": "real_word_frac: generated words that appear in the training text of any book. Verse lengths count only verses finished within 400 characters, so very long verses are under-represented."
  },
